Henry J Cobb wrote:
> Polypet doesn't break any conflicts and (at least for a wizard) can be 
> repeated until something nice comes up.

Can be repeated with any class if you have at least one of; MR, or
polycontrol, or a magic whistle. (Maybe more choices, but the above
are the most apparent and riskless ones.)

But I want to add another point to consider; it often happened to me
that even an overly powerful pet got killed literally in a single (or
very few) turn(s), so there are already other limiting factors.
Examples for that are; engulfed by trappers/lurkers, pets swallowing
a shape-changer or are bitten by green slime, iron golems turning to
dust in a rust trap, the intermediate (weak) form explodes or gets
killed by a random monster (or thrown missile), and surely some more
that I don't recall off the top of my head.

> Polymorphing yourself is an abuse [...]

I really don't think we should discuss *that*, but want to point out
at least about the polyself necessity[*] in the Rogue's quest. (No,
I don't think this is an abuse at all.)

[*] There are also a few other means to solve that level, but these
are so rare at that stage of the game that you can be really happy
if you got a way to solve it.

> The easiest way to keep a low level character from doing the dragon 
> protection scheme is to make all random polymorphs use the current 
> level's random monster creation table.

I don't think it's wrong to get a good pet. But I think indeed that
the "repeat until the pet is convenient" should be limited somewhat.
(BTW, I do that polypet orgy all the time if I can.)

My thoughts in the past about that have rather been along the line
to make the pet less tame with every shape change, thus risking that
it goes feral in the process if one does it too often.

